<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Undavalli Caves</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Undavalli Caves are rock-cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> cave temples carved into a sandstone hill on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayawada">Vijayawada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Generally attributed to the period of the Vishnukundina kings in the 5th to 7th centuries, the largest is a four-storeyed cave containing a huge reclining image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> carved from a single block of rock.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/undavalli-caves.jpg" alt="Undavalli Caves" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laxmanvety · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Undavalli_Caves,_Vijayawada,_Guntur,_Andhra_Pradesh,_India_(2018)_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The caves were carved out of solid sandstone on a hillside, primarily in the era of the Vishnukundina dynasty. The main cave is one of the earliest examples of Gupta architecture, beginning as simple rock-cut monastery cells. The caves were first shaped as a Jain abode, and part of the complex keeps the Jain style, with figures of Jain monastics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">tirthankara</a> sculptures; the site also served as a monastic complex for bhikkhus, and the carved vihara includes Buddhist artwork.</p><p>The caves lie south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayawada">Vijayawada</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntur">Guntur</a> district, within the Mangalagiri Tadepalle Municipal Corporation, and are one of the centrally protected Monuments of National Importance. From the hill above the caves, overlooking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-river">Krishna River</a>, many examples of rock-cut architecture can be seen, and the caves are surrounded by green countryside. Their walls display sculptures carved by skilled craftsmen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The largest cave is a temple of four storeys with an east-facing facade 29 metres long and 16 metres wide, each floor differing in depth. The ground floor is an unfinished low hall with eight pillars and seven door openings. The first storey has a triple shrine at the back, each with a pillared hall in front, originally dedicated to the Trinity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>, and its wall sculptures represent Vaishnava deities.</p><p>The second storey holds a pillared rectangular shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> on a serpent, a reclining statue 5 metres long. Sculptures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and of Vaishnava figures such as the Alwars were carved later, and some of the sculpture is attributed to the Chalukyan period. The top floor, with another triple shrine, was left unfinished.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Undavalli Caves” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rock-cut cave temples at <b>Undavalli</b> and Mogalrajapuram near Vijayawada are attributed to their period.</blockquote><a href="/state/vishnukundina-dynasty">Vishnukundina dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
